    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal pelvis. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 15-year-old patient presents with chronic, dull knee pain. Radiographs reveal a well-demarcated, small lytic lesion exclusively located within the epiphysis of the proximal tibia. Biopsy shows ''chicken-wire'' calcifications and mononuclear cells. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Chondroblastoma", "Enchondroma", "Osteosarcoma", "Chondromyxoid fibroma", "Giant Cell Tumor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Chondroblastoma is a rare, benign cartilage-forming tumor that characteristically occurs in the epiphysis (or apophysis) of long bones in skeletally immature patients (unlike Giant Cell Tumors, which usually occur after physeal closure). Histology classically shows ''''chicken-wire'''' calcifications.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal pelvis. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 27-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the femur that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Radiation therapy", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Open en bloc resection"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 56-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the radius. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 40-year-old patient presents with a swollen, deformed hand. Radiographs show multiple centrally located radiolucent lesions with stippled calcifications within the phalanges and metacarpals. Physical exam reveals multiple soft tissue hemangiomas on the same limb. What is the diagnosis?', '["Neurofibromatosis type 1", "Multiple hereditary exostoses", "Ollier disease", "Maffucci syndrome", "McCune-Albright syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Enchondromas are benign cartilage tumors of the medullary cavity, frequently found in the small bones of the hands and feet. Multiple enchondromas alone characterize Ollier disease. When multiple enchondromas are accompanied by soft tissue hemangiomas, it is called Maffucci syndrome, which carries a much higher risk of malignant transformation to chondrosarcoma and other visceral malignancies.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old patient presents with back pain and fatigue. Radiographs of the skull and spine show multiple ''punched-out'' radiolucencies without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. Laboratory tests reveal hypercalcemia, anemia, and an M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. Which bone scan finding is expected?', '["Diffuse periosteal uptake", "A ''superscan'' with absent renal visualization", "A falsely negative (''cold'' or normal) bone scan", "Increased uptake exclusively in the appendicular skeleton", "Intensely increased uptake (''hot'' spots) in all lesions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Multiple myeloma lesions are purely osteolytic, caused by myeloma cells stimulating osteoclasts (via RANKL) and actively inhibiting osteoblasts (via DKK-1). Because a Technetium-99m bone scan relies on osteoblastic activity (bone formation), it is notoriously unreliable (falsely negative) in multiple myeloma. A skeletal survey (plain films) or low-dose whole-body CT is the preferred imaging modality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 46-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal femur. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old boy falls and sustains a pathologic fracture of the proximal humerus. Radiographs show a centrally located, lytic lesion in the metaphysis that is well-marginated. A fragment of cortical bone is seen resting at the bottom of the cystic cavity. What is the most likely diagnosis?', '["Aneurysmal Bone Cyst", "Osteosarcoma", "Fibrous dysplasia", "Non-ossifying fibroma", "Unicameral (Simple) Bone Cyst"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The ''''fallen leaf'''' or ''''fallen fragment'''' sign is pathognomonic for a Unicameral Bone Cyst (UBC). When a pathologic fracture occurs, a piece of cortical bone breaks off and sinks through the serous fluid to the bottom of the cyst. An ABC would be eccentric, expansile, and contain blood.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old female with a history of breast cancer presents with a pathologic fracture of the proximal humerus. The lesion is lytic. What is the primary goal of orthopedic stabilization in this setting?', '["Curative resection of the tumor with wide margins", "Delaying surgery until radiation therapy is completed", "Achieving a biologic union of the fracture site without cement", "Immediate pain relief, early mobilization, and restoration of function", "Preventing pulmonary metastasis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The treatment of metastatic pathologic fractures is generally palliative. The goals are to provide immediate mechanical stability (often using intramedullary nails and cement), relieve pain, and allow early weight-bearing and functional independence.'),
