    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the humerus. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts."]'::jsonb, 4, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 34-year-old patient requires an open incisional biopsy for a suspected high-grade soft tissue sarcoma of the anterior thigh. Which of the following is a critical principle when performing this biopsy?', '["Exsanguinate the limb with an Esmarch bandage before inflating the tourniquet", "Use a transverse incision parallel to skin tension lines for a better cosmetic result", "Use a longitudinal incision placed within the planned definitive resection ellipse", "Dissect extensively through intermuscular planes to avoid muscle damage", "Obtain a small sample of the necrotic center rather than the periphery to ensure a diagnosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Orthopedic oncology biopsy principles: 1) Longitudinal incisions (transverse incisions contaminate more tissue longitudinally). 2) The biopsy tract must be excisable en bloc with the tumor. 3) Avoid spreading through fascial planes (split muscle instead). 4) Do NOT exsanguinate with an Esmarch to prevent pushing tumor cells centrally. 5) Biopsy the periphery (the advancing edge), as the center is often necrotic.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-year-old patient presents with a slowly worsening ache in the proximal humerus. Radiographs show a large, poorly defined intramedullary lesion with endosteal scalloping and prominent ''popcorn'' or ''ring-and-arc'' calcifications. Biopsy confirms malignant cartilage. What is the primary modality of treatment?', '["Systemic chemotherapy", "Denosumab therapy", "Wide surgical resection", "High-dose radiation therapy", "Radiofrequency ablation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Chondrosarcomas are malignant tumors of cartilage typically seen in older adults (pelvis, proximal femur). They are notoriously resistant to both chemotherapy and radiation therapy. Therefore, wide surgical excision is the mainstay of treatment for conventional chondrosarcomas.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 23-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the pelvis. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Necrotic tumor debris", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 8-year-old child presents with a limp. Radiographs of the proximal femur show a ''shepherd''''s crook'' deformity with an intramedullary lesion demonstrating a ''ground-glass'' appearance. The patient also has large, irregular cafe-au-lait spots with ''coast of Maine'' borders. What other clinical feature might this patient exhibit?', '["Multiple lipomas", "Hearing loss and cranial nerve palsies", "Retinal hemangioblastomas", "Axillary freckling and Lisch nodules", "Precocious puberty"]'::jsonb, 4, 'McCune-Albright syndrome consists of the triad of polyostotic fibrous dysplasia (ground-glass bone lesions, shepherd''''s crook deformity), café-au-lait macules with irregular borders (coast of Maine), and endocrine abnormalities (most commonly precocious puberty). It is caused by an activating mutation in the GNAS gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old patient presents with back pain and fatigue. Radiographs of the skull and spine show multiple ''punched-out'' radiolucencies without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. Laboratory tests reveal hypercalcemia, anemia, and an M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. Which bone scan finding is expected?', '["Intensely increased uptake (''hot'' spots) in all lesions", "A falsely negative (''cold'' or normal) bone scan", "A ''superscan'' with absent renal visualization", "Increased uptake exclusively in the appendicular skeleton", "Diffuse periosteal uptake"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Multiple myeloma lesions are purely osteolytic, caused by myeloma cells stimulating osteoclasts (via RANKL) and actively inhibiting osteoblasts (via DKK-1). Because a Technetium-99m bone scan relies on osteoblastic activity (bone formation), it is notoriously unreliable (falsely negative) in multiple myeloma. A skeletal survey (plain films) or low-dose whole-body CT is the preferred imaging modality.'),
