    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-year-old patient presents with back pain and fatigue. Radiographs of the skull and spine show multiple ''punched-out'' radiolucencies without any surrounding reactive sclerosis. Laboratory tests reveal hypercalcemia, anemia, and an M-spike on serum protein electrophoresis. Which bone scan finding is expected?', '["Diffuse periosteal uptake", "Increased uptake exclusively in the appendicular skeleton", "A falsely negative (''cold'' or normal) bone scan", "A ''superscan'' with absent renal visualization", "Intensely increased uptake (''hot'' spots) in all lesions"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Multiple myeloma lesions are purely osteolytic, caused by myeloma cells stimulating osteoclasts (via RANKL) and actively inhibiting osteoblasts (via DKK-1). Because a Technetium-99m bone scan relies on osteoblastic activity (bone formation), it is notoriously unreliable (falsely negative) in multiple myeloma. A skeletal survey (plain films) or low-dose whole-body CT is the preferred imaging modality.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 13-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the radius. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Separation of cellular blood components from serum", "Necrotic tumor debris", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Pus settling from an abscess"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 35-year-old patient presents with a slowly growing, deep, painless mass in the popliteal fossa. MRI shows a heterogeneous mass near the knee joint, but not within the joint capsule. Biopsy confirms a soft tissue sarcoma. Genetic testing reveals a t(X;18) chromosomal translocation. What is the diagnosis?', '["Undifferentiated pleomorphic sarcoma", "Synovial Sarcoma", "Rhabdomyosarcoma", "Liposarcoma", "Clear cell sarcoma"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Despite its name, Synovial Sarcoma rarely arises from the actual synovium of the joint, but typically occurs near large joints (e.g., the knee) in young adults. It is strongly associated with the t(X;18) translocation, which creates the SS18-SSX fusion gene.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the radius. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Osteoblast overactivation", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass", "Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 64-year-old patient complains of severe pain in the humerus. Imaging shows a ''punched-out'' lytic lesion. Blood work reveals anemia, hypercalcemia, and renal insufficiency. A serum protein electrophoresis (SPEP) is ordered. What is the expected mechanism of the bone destruction?', '["Neoplastic plasma cells secrete factors (like MIP-1 alpha and RANKL) that stimulate osteoclasts.", "Deposition of amyloid protein in the bone matrix", "Osteoblast overactivation", "Ischemic necrosis of the medullary cavity", "Direct mechanical pressure from the tumor mass"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In multiple myeloma, malignant plasma cells upregulate osteoclast activity (via RANKL and MIP-1 alpha) and downregulate osteoblast activity (via DKK-1), leading to pure lytic lesions without reactive new bone formation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 17-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the pelvis that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open en bloc resection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Radiation therapy", "Cryoablation without imaging", "Intra-articular steroid injection"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 12-year-old patient has intense nocturnal pain in the pelvis that responds dramatically to naproxen. Imaging confirms a nidus with surrounding sclerosis. If the patient opts for minimally invasive intervention, what is the treatment of choice?', '["Cryoablation without imaging", "Intra-articular steroid injection", "Open en bloc resection", "CT-guided radiofrequency ablation (RFA)", "Radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Osteoid osteomas are benign bone-forming tumors. While they can ''''burn out'''' spontaneously over years, symptomatic patients who cannot tolerate long-term NSAIDs are best treated with percutaneous, CT-guided radiofrequency ablation, which has a very high success rate.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 22-year-old patient presents with rapid swelling of the femur. Radiographs show an eccentric, expansile metaphyseal lesion. MRI shows multiple fluid-fluid levels. What does this MRI finding represent?', '["Necrotic tumor debris", "Fat floating on water within a lipoma", "Pus settling from an abscess", "Contrast pooling in a hemangioma", "Separation of cellular blood components from serum"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The ''''fluid-fluid level'''' on MRI in an aneurysmal bone cyst (ABC) represents the settling of red blood cells out of serum in the cyst''''s cavernous, blood-filled spaces.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 21-year-old patient is diagnosed with high-grade conventional osteosarcoma of the distal radius. They are scheduled for a limb-salvage procedure. According to standard protocol, what must precede the surgical resection?', '["Amputation", "Denosumab", "Neoadjuvant chemotherapy", "Bisphosphonates", "Radiation therapy"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Standard treatment for high-grade osteosarcoma includes neoadjuvant (preoperative) chemotherapy, which targets micrometastases and shrinks the tumor, followed by wide surgical resection and adjuvant chemotherapy.');
